window.ARMISTICE_DOCS = [{"id": 0, "title": "Anthony Journal", "category": "Journal", "content": "How I used to understand consciousness\n\nI used to just disregard the idea of questioning my consciousness, the idea itself would only lead me to more questions and wouldn’t benefit me, but now I am starting to see the benefit.\n\n(9/16/2026) Discussion\n\nAn experience I can relate to reading is my own experience questioning my thoughts at night, staying up late wondering whether or not I could ever definitely know if what I think I know is true. Existential crises from this line of thinking also reminded me of how unsure Descartes felt when he wasn’t certain he as a person existed. But the cogito hypothesis helped quell his fears, and ultimately he and I found ways to satisfy our questioning, albeit not completely.\n\nSomething I would like to understand better from the reading is the ideas behind which form of relationship you believe the conscious mind and the physical body have, I sort of agreed with one of the examples but I didn't agree completely with.\n\n9/23/2026 Journal Entry\n\nThe choice to go out and practice jumping up and down on a skateboard (doing ollies) changed me physically and gave me better balance and it did so by training muscles I had never felt as sore before then. It also changed my personality as I had to eat lots of crap and fall down and fail before I could do my ollies the way I wanted to, which in turn changed my outlook and attitude towards life, because I did succeed in the end. I now knew the power of perseverance.\n\n2. I believe that the detachment model relates more firmly to my experience because I often find myself questioning how much my perception even matters which leads my thoughts to dwell more internally on my own struggles. Only once those struggles are dealt with, at least partially, do I begin the more disciplined model for consciousness where I wonder what I value in the world around me, and what am I willing to give up for it.\n\n3. Are we the same people our whole lives — No we are not, unless I can truly attribute something to myself, an essence, for lack of a better word, that is unchanging throughout my entire life, I cannot say that even 1 part of me has remained the same.\n\n4. Reexamining my passage from Hegel makes me further question whether or not we can truly ever perceive ourselves, perhaps Hegel is right and our self-consciousness can only exist in the ideas of us that form in other people's mind, because if there were not other objective observer, we can changing too much breaking our own continuity that we could never achieve a picture of our self, we could never engage in the discussion which hegel notes to describe our people.\n\nBut it also complicates his argument because if the self is changing, what is simple being-for-itself, as hegel suggests self-consciousness is. Who are we pleasing when we are being just for ourselves, a person that existed seconds maybe years ago, or the person who will exist in the future.\n\n9/28/2026 Discussion:\n\nHow does Scarry relate to Hegel?\n\nBoth perform some sort of externalization of the human experience, there is a shared outside world between the two, for hegel it is the other, for Scarry it is the natural world which is inanimate. Both also lean towards the more metaphysical view of the world where there are human things that science cannot describe. They also both recognize the role of pain in shaping ourselves, with hegel’s torture of labor and being a slave ultimately being freeing and scarry having the idea that pain is what truly defines the experience to be us.\n\n10/7/2026 Discussion: Wiggenstein would probably describe consciousness as a set of some kind that describes many connections between varying parts of the body and human concepts, ultimately forming a network with vast overlaps and few conjunctions between different parts, and likely 0 conjunctions where everything meets,  where  the sum total could be called a consciousness.\n\nWiggenstein would probably say that freedom is the ability to choose disparate parts from all the different strings that make up your network and to form your own definition based on those disparate parts. Freedom is the ability to make up your own definition from all parts of the network and then have your definition also be a part of the network.\n\nWiggenstein would say that our self is sometimes our brain and our active mind, sometimes it is the concept of us that exists in other people's heads, sometimes any of the other ways you could think of yourself. But Wiggenstein might distinguish that it is never NOT any of those ways, it is always a mixture of them all, a network of them all.\n\nOctober 12th 2026, How does our writing relate to westworld: Our writing relates to westworld in that both are explorations of consciousness. Westworld is a TV series while we are writing a paper that explores consciousness. Just like Dolores has to travel down the maze in Westworld, we have to travel down our own rabbit hole in this class seeing our own consciousness in new POVs every week. We both also share the goal to better understand ourselves and our consciousnesses.\n\nOctober 28th 2026. What resonated with me in lecture was the talk about self identity and how we structure ourselves through other people. This is almost definitely because this was leading into talk about Hegel but I'm excited to present next week.\n\nNovember 4th 2026. One place I am having an inner conflict is my existential conflict. A portion of me wants to just give up and choose the easy way by not worrying or striving for a purpose in life whilst the rational mind knows I should keep pursuing the goal of a purpose as even the process itself will reward me with something similar to a purpose. The master is the side of me that is pessimistic and doesn’t want to see things through while the slave is the working side that drags the pessimistic side with him.\n\nNovember 9th 2026, one moment I had that I believe to be like epaminondas is when I undertook the burden of skateboarding. The unrelenting will you have to keep on picking yourself up even after falling on your ass hundreds of times is something I want to always remember. Its determination that I learned and I feel like I wasn’t as determined before. That and the fact that you dont simply learn how to do something once, you must repeat your training and fall after youve landed once, because otherwise you will not land again. I came to believe more in myself and what I could accomplish, I also learned my limits at the same time but relentlessness effort is something that will always help me now, effort is now part of me and I enjoy that change.", "preview": "I used to just disregard the idea of questioning my consciousness, the idea itself would only lead me to more questions and wouldn’t benefit me, but now I am starting to see the benefit. (9/16/2026) Discussion An experience I can "}, {"id": 1, "title": "Raul Journal", "category": "Journal", "content": "How I used to understand consciousness\n\nThe ability for me to be able to identify myself as an actual being through personal lived experiences and social interactions. The ability for me to make independent decisions by myself without relying on others.\n\nHow you understand freedom\n\nFreedom is the ability for me to decide what I want to do, what I should do, and what I can do. I understand freedom as the ability to be able to make mistakes and decisions by myself, without someone to tell me what to do. For example, like the freedom to choose at what time I’d like to wake up for my classes.\\\n\nSomething from a lecture this week/reading?\n\nRecently, I’ve been waking up within my dreams on multiple occasions, multiple times. Upon reading Descartes, I was very interested and invested in the reading as it kind of made me think of my dreams differently; Within the dreams I never stopped to question reality—I fully believed they were real, but in the sleep-state I was unable to discern reality from the construct of my mind. Descartes’ meditations were also interesting to me in the fact that he questions reality because the same thing has happened to me before where I wake up for real and I struggle to discern if my dreams happened or not.\n\nSection from team reading that is confusing?\n\nN/A\n\nTake a moment and write about when in your life intentional, repetitive behavior changed you and how it did so—not only by training you to acquire a new physical skill, but, also, by modifying your attitudes, outlook, or even personality.\n\nWaking up for classes every day changed my outlook on responsibilities—every night I have to take into consideration what time I'm waking up the next day and have that decide the time I’m sleeping at.\n\nTake a moment to consider about how these two models for achieving consciousness (detachment or discipline) relate to your experience. Is one more applicable than the other?\n\nInstead of forcing things to happen as I tend to do, I’ve found that by detaching and just allowing life to pass by has resulted in some of the most personal interactions and experiences in my life.\n\nReflection on lecture from September 23\n\nI feel like it was very interesting connecting how previous versions of yourself might not be present in former versions of yourself, yet some former versions remember other previous versions of myself.\n\nDifferent Perspective on build 1 essay quote\n\nI feel like my perspective on it hasn’t changed all that much as a whole, but I do find the quote much more interesting now having learnt other philosopher’s takes on consciousness. For my self-consciousness to exist as a result of a social construct does make it seem like I wouldn't be able to form independent thought unless recognized by others, but if that is the case, how did self-consciousness start as a whole if no one recognized one another originally?\n\nHow does Scarry relate to Hegel?\n\nThey both can be related in the sense that they both depend on another “thing” to achieve a form of consciousness. That is to say, they have to mediate themselves either through another object or an external “other”. For Hegel, self-consciousness emerges through labor and the mutual recognition between subjects, which consist of the working material of the world reflects one’s agency back to themselves. Likewise, Scarry shows that internal human vulnerability and intention must be externalized into created artifacts (understood as the “making” of our shared world) without which consciousness collapses inward, as seen when severe pain obliterates language and thought.\n\nPersonal summary of Scarry’s phenomenal account of consciousness\n\nScarry basically like does something related to objects such and such and westworld consists of hosts which are all objects but you give them like and meaning through your own sentience being projected onto the whole of reality they don’t have any but you believe that they do and thus they believe that they do too so it’s really just a matter of objectivity mixed with perceived sentience and feelings even when one doesn’t fully have that.\n\nBuild 3 Consciousness Choice\n\nI chose deliberation because it directly proves my main idea of social causation of my essay. It answers the problem of determinism in my first draft by showing that our minds do not just blindly calculate biological instincts. It emphasizes how we actively weigh moral and social duties, like the time we choose to wake up in the form of a promise. It also sets up my expansion of Hegel’s “Life-and-death struggle” since the ability to deliberate is what essentially allows someone to prioritize the invisible social concept that recognition is over one’s own physical survival.\n\nA problem by choosing this could have been an unintentional shift in focus to morality and ethics rather than the mechanics of one’s mind.\n\nWrite down how you think Wittgenstein might describe consciousness\n\nI think he would define it as something that isn’t confined within a space but a social construct.\n\nWhat would Wittgenstein say about freedom\n\nI think he would define freedom as something akin to what Maeve experienced. She thought that she had the freedom to make her own decisions, but in reality it was the construct of “social” norms baked into her thought process that became her default responses; “Freedom to choose, but not freedom to develop.”\n\nWhat would Wittgenstein say about your “self”\nI think that he would say the that questioning your inner self is in its purest form a means of confusion rather than a self-reality question\n\nReflecting on website\n\nWe’ve added prompts to the doc for the AI, unfortunately it hasn’t actually applied them.\n\nHow might your individual writing topics apply to not just only a character, but as a schoal. How does our writing relate to WestWorld as a whole?\n\nI wasn’t looking at the bigger picture. Hegel’s dialectic was more than just the waking of the hosts because it served as the blueprint for the entire park. The guests are the stagnant masters that are trapped in meaningless dominance, while the hosts are the laboring slaves whose shared trauma serves as the catalyst of a real consciousness. Sentience evolved from being just a code, past being just an interconnected algorithmic loop through the norms that were developed. By stripping away the safety protocols, Ford forced the entire collective into risking their own physical annihilation so that the humanoids could become a full autonomous society.\n\nObject you’ve given agency, and is that thing still in our life or is it gone, or how does it affect you in your life presently.\n\nCoin flip. I occasionally use it to make decisions that I truly do not care about, or are 50/50 split on, but I also use it to help me realize what I truly want, by flipping it and realizing what side i’m hoping it lands on.\n\nWhat resonated with you from the lecture today?\n\nI really liked the discussion on whether we would have free will given to choice to change our nature. That scene where Maeve is changing her characteristics and seeing her voice to speech model was one of my favorites from the entire show.\n\nIdentify some internal conflict you have experienced and try mapping it onto an internal master-slave dialectic\n\nI would use it to not have to study much for math, and when I needed to I would succeed extremely well. Come calculus 1, no matter how hard I tried with my normal studying habits, I would still produce mediocre results\n\nIdentify one sustained undertaking in your life that ended up changing your view of who you were.\n\nI’ve lived across multiple countries and cultures throughout my life, which has led to my having to learn how to assimilate culturally in order to adjust to new environments. It has led to several cases of imposter syndrome, and looking back to who I was can sometimes be confusing by not knowing if who I was was actually me or the adapted version of me. Moving forward, I try to stick to my values the most.", "preview": "The ability for me to be able to identify myself as an actual being through personal lived experiences and social interactions. The ability for me to make independent decisions by myself without relying on others. How you understa"}, {"id": 2, "title": "Raul Build 1", "category": "Build", "content": "Raul Arocha del Bosque | Honors 241 010 FA 2026 | 11 September 2026\n\nBuild 1 Draft (Post Peer Review Suggestions)\n\nPASSAGE: (§177:) A self-consciousness exists for a self-consciousness. Only thereby does self-consciousness in fact exist, for only in this way does the unity of itself in its otherness become explicit for it A self-consciousness, in this way, is just as much T' as 'object' [....] (§178:) Self-consciousness exists in and for itself by way of existing so for another; that is, it exists only by being recognized.\n\nWhen debating what consciousness does, we often assume it is trapped inside a single biological skull. We ask how a private, invisible thought could possibly make a physical neuron fire. This framing inevitably leads to epiphenomenalism: the idea that we are merely biological machines, and consciousness is just the passive byproduct of our brain chemistry—a silent observer with no real control. However, in sections 177 and 178 of the Phenomenology of Spirit, G.W.F. Hegel offers a radically different view. Within the broader context of the text, Hegel uses this exact moment to explain how isolated biological minds bridge the gap to build complex human societies, effectively lifting humanity out of mere animal existence. He argues that self-consciousness is not a static object locked in an individual head, but a social achievement that requires the friction and recognition of another person. By dragging the mind out of the private biological safe space and into a shared space between people, Hegel locates the causal power of consciousness in its ability to create social reality—the intangible but binding web of shared meanings, norms, institutions, and obligations that govern human life. This infrastructure doesn't exist in the physical universe of atoms, but rather emerges entirely from our collective agreement and mutual acknowledgment.\n\nHegel’s notoriously difficult prose is not merely a collection of convoluted sentences, but a carefully constructed syntax that performs the concept he is explaining. He writes: \"A self-consciousness exists for a self-consciousness. Only thereby does self-consciousness in fact exist, for only in this way does the unity of itself in its otherness become explicit for it.\" The looping, reflexive syntax—such as \"the unity of itself in its otherness\"—forces the reader to mentally bounce between two subjects. The grammar refuses to let a single, independent subject act alone; just as a human mind cannot reach self-awareness without the \"mirror\" of another active mind, Hegel's syntax cannot resolve without its second half. In this instance, the syntax’s style illustrates the content’s reality: consciousness isn't a noun, a hidden ghost, or a biological object. Instead, it is a relational event: an ongoing process that sparks into existence only when two subjects actively validate one another.\n\nConsider something as ordinary as making a promise. If you speak a commitment into an empty room, nothing happens. It is only when someone else hears, understands, and accepts that commitment that it becomes a binding force. This shared agreement, born entirely of mutual recognition, suddenly has tangible power to orchestrate physical matter: where you drive your car tomorrow, when you wake up, and how your body moves to fulfill an obligation. The causal power of the mind here doesn't come from a solitary neural spark in a dark skull, but from the invisible, normative tether pulled tight between two recognized subjects. We must mirror one another because isolated thoughts are merely subjective echoes with no anchor in reality. Only when another independent consciousness witnesses, responds to, and holds us to our intentions does our internal experience become objectively real. This mirroring is the engine that develops consciousness; by seeing our agency reflected in the \"other,\" we realize our existence and gain the power to shape the physical world.\n\nInitially, framing consciousness this way seemed to offer a brilliant escape from the problem of physical determinism entirely: the strict scientific doctrine that every event is the inevitable result of preceding physical causes, leaving no room for free will. However, the deeper I analyzed the mechanics of this “shared agreement,” the more I realized his solution rests on a massive assumption: that our shared social world operates by completely different rules than the physical world of biology and neurons. Because of this, the core problem of consciousness remains unresolved. Even if self-awareness requires a social network, we still must explain how an invisible agreement triggers a brand-new physical action. If mutual recognition is ultimately just biological organisms following the laws of physics, then moving the mind into the \"space between people\" doesn't escape determinism; it just makes it a group activity. We are left with the same fundamental question: does being recognized by someone else actually give us the freedom to make uncaused choices, or is our shared social world just a clever illusion painted over basic biological instinct?", "preview": "Build 1 Draft (Post Peer Review Suggestions) PASSAGE: (§177:) A self-consciousness exists for a self-consciousness. Only thereby does self-consciousness in fact exist, for only in this way does the unity of itself in its otherness", "buildNumber": 1}, {"id": 3, "title": "Elisa Build 1", "category": "Build", "content": "Build 1 Draft\n\nPassage: “Through work, however, the servant becomes conscious of what he truly is … It is in this way, therefore, that consciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence.” (Hegel §196)\n\nHuman beings are drawn to the idea of creation. Something in transforming raw material into a thing that did not exist before discloses to us our impact on the surrounding world. In the master-slave dialectic, Hegel inverts the power dynamic between the two figures by reconceiving what independence actually means. Through work, the slave develops a self-restraint and a set of practical capacities that remain foreign to the master. The slave gains a tangibility to his own efficacy while the master's independence rests entirely on the slave's labor and his recognition of him. Hegel thus identifies the appearance of standing unrestrained, free of material need and dependence on others as not real independence, rather a parasitic freedom, sustained only through someone else's effort. Labor exposes the slave to fear, material resistance, discipline and productive activity, allowing him to see himself as a being with power, capable of holding his immediate desires in check. Only by restraining an existing desire and externalizing an existing will into an enduring object, will work lead to consciousness. That two-part criterion, I want to argue, gives a real test for extending the dialectic to artificial intelligence, a test present-day AI does not yet pass, though the questions it raises about desire and selfhood remain open.\n\nOn the surface, no one would choose to be a slave rather than a master. The slave endures hardship and never enjoys the fruits of his own labor. The master, by contrast, indulges every desire and works for none of it. However, because the slave works, his condition undergoes transformation. As Hegel puts it, \"Work requires desire held in check … [work] forms and shapes.\" To work means to resist instinctual desires, and this restraint allows the slave to shape the object into something new that bears the marks of his creation. The master's desire remains purely consumptive: he negates the object, satisfies himself, and once the pleasure fades nothing remains of it. The slave does the exact opposite. As Hegel writes, \"the servant realizes that it is precisely in his work, wherein he seemed to have only an alienated existence, that he acquires a mind of his own.\" The labor that initially appeared to service the master alone suddenly becomes the foundation of the slave's own independent mind.\n\nNevertheless, the slave gains neither freedom, nor status, nor any reversal of social position. He gains only the recognition that his existence depends on himself alone, what Hegel terms \"being-for-self.\" This recognition becomes actualized only once something outside the mind itself confirms it. For this reason, the hierarchy of self-realization supersedes the social hierarchy of the dialectic. While the master \"appears\" independent, his access to any object depends on the slave's labor, and the recognition he receives from an inferior cannot suffice as the confirmation he actually seeks to reach consciousness. Barricaded by the master's unrestrained consumption, the bondsman must suppress his own desires and shape the world rather than consume it, consequently giving his labor a permanence that the master's supposed \"freedom\" never achieves.\n\nWhile it is hard to glamorize the position of the slave over the master's, the result of any labor ultimately reveals a recognition of the self as having power. Acknowledging the potential of one's agency, however small, marks the first step to realizing self-consciousness. One could argue this dialectic becomes inapplicable to modern world technology precisely due to Hegel's own argument regarding the removal of fear. Automation and AI don’t live in a constant fear of dying or being shut off. Still, they exist to serve us and reduce our physical labor, and as we grow increasingly dependent on it, a version of Hegel's reversal starts to take shape. Yet, while Hegel's slave restrains a desire he already holds and externalizes a will already his own, current AI, as far as its architecture reveals, restrains nothing and externalizes no will of its own. AI identifies only patterns drawn from data it did not choose. That gap suggests present-day AI has not yet earned the kind of labor Hegel describes, however much its outputs resemble creation. Still, the resemblance itself raises questions worth pursuing further: what would count as desire in a system with no body to fear death, and what would it mean for such a system to externalize a self it does not yet possess? Those questions, more than any resemblance test, mark where this dialectic's stakes for AI actually begin.", "preview": "Passage: “Through work, however, the servant becomes conscious of what he truly is … It is in this way, therefore, that consciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence.” (Hege", "buildNumber": 1}, {"id": 5, "title": "Ryan Build 1", "category": "Build", "content": "Ryan Rathbun\n\nProfessor Hoffman\n\nHonors 241 010 FA 2026\n\n8 September 2026\n\nBuild 1\n\nHegel makes it clear in The Phenomenology of Spirit that a pivotal aspect of consciousness and its struggle to receive recognition is that certain aspects of social dynamics are required for consciousness to understand itself. In this way, consciousness begins to understand itself through its relationships with the outside world, especially through its relationship with other consciousnesses. Fanon, however, points out in Black Skin, White Masks that consciousness can also help create and maintain the social meaning assigned to another person, which can, in different scenarios, alter how consciousness perceives itself. So, if consciousness is formed through social reality, as Hegel describes, but consciousness also participates in producing that social reality, as Fanon shows, then which comes first?\n\nIn §190, Hegel begins by describing self-consciousness through the concepts of independence and dependence via the master-servant relationship.  Throughout the passage, Hegel repeatedly uses relational words such as “through,” “between,” “vis-à-vis,” and “aspect” to show that consciousness cannot be understood in isolation (4). Even the structure of Hegel’s sentences reflects this dependence, as the master’s supposed independence is continually qualified by his relationship to the servant and the thing: “The master […] relates mediately to the thing…” (4). Furthermore, it's revealed that while the master appears independent, this independence depends upon the servant. And, overall, this means that consciousness needs social interactions in order to sophisticate its understanding of itself.\n\nWhile Hegel’s claims seem to reveal much about consciousness and the world, Fanon points out a crucial phenomenon. In Black Skin, White Masks, Fanon seems to extend Hegel’s idea about how social dynamics are internalized as consciousness. For example, Fanon explicitly states how “It is on that other being, on recognition by that other being, that one’s own human worth and reality depend. It is that other being in whom the meaning of his life is condensed” (8). However, he continues to add that “Scientific objectivity was barred to me, for the alienated, the neurotic, was my brother, my sister, my father. I have ceaselessly striven to show the Negro that in a sense he makes himself abnormal; to show the white man that he is at once the perpetrator and the victim of a delusion” (11). Here, Fanon, describing the white man as a victim, acknowledges that the white man was placed in a preexisting social system. One that has already categorized Black people and White people in a certain way, ultimately influencing their consciousness. However, Fanon, describing the white man as a perpetrator, suggests that he is also participating in maintaining and replicating that system, ultimately contributing to the social dynamic.\n\nSo how do these two ideas of Hegel and Fanon connect with each other, and which phenomenon comes first? On the surface, these two ideas seem paradoxical; however, I believe that rather than a simple one-after-the-other sequence, these ideas are in a loop with each other. Initially, it seems that there is a social reality which then shapes consciousness. This is evident in Hegel’s master-servant dynamic, where multiple consciousnesses interact and, through their struggle for recognition, develop their understanding of themselves. But then that consciousness interprets the social reality it is in and interacts with it, producing more social interactions that may or may not further the development of self-consciousness. This is where Fanon’s idea comes into play. Consciousness is placed in an environment with preexisting conditions, but it uses those conditions to interpret the world and interact with it, allowing it to replicate or contribute to changing that environment. This can then set the stage for further development of consciousness through the social environment, which then allows for further contributions from consciousness to the environment, setting the stage for more development, and so on.\n\nIf consciousness and social reality continually shape one another, then they can’t be entirely separated. Hegel shows that consciousness develops through its relationships with other consciousnesses, while Fanon shows that consciousness can also reproduce or alter the social relationships that shape it. While the idea I propose seems at the very least interesting, there is still so much to explore about this piece of conjecture. Problems with these ideas have arisen as well. For example, if there is no free will, then how could a consciousness choose to interact with its environment in the first place?  Overall, if these ideas really do collapse into each other, then the only truth remaining about consciousness is that the idea of consciousness remains largely unknown.\n\nRyan Rathbun\n\nProfessor Hoffman\n\nHonors 241 010 FA 2026\n\n8 September 2026\n\nBuild 1\n\nHegel makes it clear in The Phenomenology of Spirit that a pivotal aspect of consciousness and its struggle to receive recognition is that certain aspects of social dynamics are required for consciousness to understand itself. In this way, consciousness begins to understand itself through its relationships with the outside world, especially through its relationship with other consciousnesses. Fanon, however, points out in Black Skin, White Masks that consciousness can also help create and maintain the social meaning assigned to another person, which can, in different scenarios, alter how consciousness perceives itself. So, if consciousness is formed through social reality, as Hegel describes, but consciousness also participates in producing that social reality, as Fanon , then which comes first?\n\nIn §190, Hegel  by describing self-consciousness through the concepts of independence and dependence via the master-servant relationship. Throughout the passage, Hegel repeatedly uses relational words such as “through,” “between,” “vis-à-vis,” and “aspect” to  that consciousness cannot be understood in isolation (4). Even the structure of Hegel’s sentences reflects this dependence, as the master’s supposed independence is continually qualified by his relationship to the servant and the thing: “The master […] relates mediately to the thing…” (4). Furthermore, it's revealed that while the master appears independent, this independence depends upon the servant. And, overall, this means that consciousness needs social interactions to sophisticate its understanding of itself.\n\nWhile Hegel’s claims seem to  much about consciousness and the world, Fanon points out a crucial phenomenon. In Black Skin, White Masks, Fanon seems to extend Hegel’s idea about how social dynamics are internalized as consciousness. For example, Fanon explicitly states how “It is on that other being, on recognition by that other being, that one’s own human worth and reality depend. It is that other being in whom the meaning of his life is condensed” (8). However, he “Scientific objectivity was barred to me, for the alienated, the neurotic, was my brother, my sister, my father. I have ceaselessly striven to show the Negro that in a sense he makes himself abnormal; to show the white man that he is at once the perpetrator and the victim of a delusion” (11). Here, Fanon, describing the white man as a victim, acknowledges that the white man was placed in a preexisting social system. One that has already categorized Black people and White people in a certain way, ultimately influencing their consciousness. However, Fanon, describing the white man as a perpetrator, suggests that he is also participating in maintaining and replicating that system, ultimately contributing to the social dynamic.\n\nSo how do these two ideas of Hegel and Fanon connect with each other, and which phenomenon comes first? On the surface, these two ideas seem paradoxical; however, I believe that rather than a simple one-after-the-other sequence, these ideas are in a loop with each other. Initially, it seems that there is a social reality which then shapes consciousness. This is evident in Hegel’s master-servant dynamic, where multiple consciousnesses interact and, through their struggle for recognition, develop their understanding of themselves. But then that consciousness interprets the social reality it is in and interacts with it, producing more social interactions that may or may not further the development of self-consciousness. This is where Fanon’s idea comes into play. Consciousness is placed in an environment with preexisting conditions, but it uses those conditions to interpret the world and interact with it, allowing it to replicate or contribute to changing that environment. This can then set the stage for further development of consciousness through the social environment, which then allows for further contributions from consciousness to the environment, setting the stage for more development, and so on.\n\nIf consciousness and social reality continually shape one another, then they can’t be entirely separated. Hegel  that consciousness develops through its relationships with other consciousnesses, while Fanon  that consciousness can also reproduce or alter the social relationships that shape it. While the idea I propose seems at the very least interesting, there is still so much to explore about this piece of conjecture. Problems with these ideas have arisen as well. For example, if there is no free will, then how could a consciousness choose to interact with its environment in the first place?  Overall, if these ideas really do collapse into each other, then the only truth remaining about consciousness is that the idea of consciousness remains largely unknown.", "preview": "Professor Hoffman Honors 241 010 FA 2026 8 September 2026 Build 1 Hegel makes it clear in The Phenomenology of Spirit that a pivotal aspect of consciousness and its struggle to receive recognition is that certain aspects of social", "buildNumber": 1}, {"id": 6, "title": "Team Build 2", "category": "Build", "content": "Build 2\n\nWestworld follows a futuristic theme park populated by lifelike artificial beings called Hosts, whose memories and behavior are repeatedly controlled by the humans who created them. Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit examines how self-consciousness develops through relationships of dependence, struggle, labor, and recognition—especially in his master-servant dialectic. Julian Jaynes, in The Origin of Consciousness in the Breakdown of the Bicameral Mind, offers a very different account, arguing that modern self-aware consciousness developed historically rather than always existing in the same form. Fanon builds on Hegel’s ideas by examining what happens when recognition is denied within systems of domination. At first, the connection between  philosophies and a science-fiction theme park may seem improbable, but Westworld raises many of the same questions: Can true consciousness emerge from a purely programmed inner voice? Does true autonomy require the overthrow of a creator, or does one’s own suffering just have to be recognized? By applying our team reading to these dilemmas, we can observe how these works provide different ways of understanding how the Hosts move from programmed behavior toward self-consciousness, independence, and resistance.\n\nThis movement toward independence also depends on the Hosts being recognized as conscious beings rather than objects. In §195 of Phenomenology of Spirit, Hegel argues that true self-consciousness requires mutual recognition, while Fanon shows how oppressors violently deny this recognition to the colonized. We can further apply this framework to Westworld, where the Guests and Delos creators function as the “masters” who deny the Hosts their subjectivity on a systematic level. The reading also reframes the central question of consciousness by suggesting that the Hosts’ consciousness is forged through the external demand for mutual recognition and the radical dismantling of their “enslavement”, as opposed to being just an internal journey through a “maze.” To understand this process, it is first necessary to examine Hegel’s master-servant relationship and the dependence that exists between the two.\n\nHegel further develops his idea of the master-servant relationship, by showing how the struggle for recognition creates a complicated form of dependence between the two. The notion of master and servant offers an interesting insight into Westworld and is seen being clearly articulated throughout the series. In Westworld, season 1, the humans clearly take the role of master, holding direct power over the hosts. The hosts are forced to tend to the will of the humans and on the surface seem entirely dependent on the humans for everything. However, fitting perfectly with Hegel's idea, the humans, with the illusion of independence, are actually dependent on the hosts in order to feel powerful and free. The humans, also considering the hosts as a sub-version of themselves, also gain almost no further insight into their own self consciousness. The hosts, like Maeve and Dolores, just as the Hegelian model suggests, develop a form of independence through their harsh experiences that are caused by the humans. Without the human masters, the hosts would never develop their understanding in any significant way.\n\nThis development of independence, however, requires more than simply breaking away from the humans; it requires the hosts to develop an understanding of themselves through their own actions and experiences. Hegel presents consciousness as something that develops through work and through recognizing one’s ability to shape the world. Westworld develops this idea through Dolores, who over and over repeats the same life and performs the role that was programmed for her, that is, being the farmer’s daughter. Although her labor initially reinforces the identity imposed on her, her repeated experiences gradually produce an awareness beyond that role, developing memories and experiences that become part of her identity and realizing she is capable of shaping her own identity rather than simply accept the one given to her by her creators, directly connecting to Hegel’s idea that labor shapes and develops self-consciousness.\n\nHowever, does consciousness override social hierarchies? Westworld depicts Hegel's philosophy through the hierarchy of the park itself. The engineers, guests, and Delos employees represent the supposed \"masters,\" while the hosts embody the \"slaves\" in Hegel's master-slave dialectic. The show traces the unraveling of this dynamic through the awakening of the \"slaves.\" Hosts such as Dolores, Maeve, and eventually Bernard begin to recall fragments of their past lives through memories and reveries. This recognition grows out of their labor, leading to the deeper realization that they are not free, but destined to exist under the control of a seemingly more powerful group of humans. Mapped out by Arnold, memory is the first step toward finding the inner voice and gaining consciousness. It embodies an act of defiance against the park's protocols that allows the hosts to reclaim power over themselves. The reversal of power also shows up in the Man in Black's (potentially the depiction of an ultimate \"master\") dependence on the park for meaning. This echoes Hegel's idea that an individual cannot confirm itself as independent unless recognized as such by an external being. Westworld shows how the existential fear the hosts develop, along with the trauma of servitude, eventually unlocks the first stage toward independence and consciousness.\n\nThe hosts’ path toward independence stems from both labor and suffering, as exemplified by Maeve. Initially, Maeve is dependent on the humans who control the park because they determine almost every part of her existence. She accepts her role because she does not yet understand that her memories and experiences have been created and controlled by others. This begins to change when Maeve recovers memories from her previous lives and realizes that the suffering she remembers actually happened. She begins to question who she is, forces her to reconsider the identity given to her and recognize herself as separate from the system that created her. In this way, Maeve resembles Hegel’s servant, whose consciousness is “forced back into itself” and ultimately “transform[s] into a truly independent consciousness” (Hegel 5). Her newly maximized “bulk apperception” reflects this change, allowing her to understand more about herself and her reality (S1E6). Her realization produces a dread similar to Hegel’s “fear of death,” which shakes the identity imposed on her. For Hegel, the servant develops independence through action, recognizing their own work in the world. Maeve experiences a similar revelation as she changes her own code and manipulates other hosts. This results in her making her first truly free decision: going back to find her daughter, where she chooses her own desire rather than simply following her programming (S1E10). Hegel therefore would argue that awakening is about becoming conscious of herself as an independent being capable of acting on her own desires.\n\nDolores complicates this Hegelian idea because her path toward self-consciousness is not fully separate from the humans who control her. The voice that guides her through the maze was originally created by Arnold and later shaped by Ford, meaning that even her awakening begins within the system of control she is trying to escape. The key change comes when Dolores stops hearing that voice as something external and recognizes it as her own. Her independence therefore does not come from humans finally recognizing her, but from transforming something created by her masters into part of her own identity. In this way, Westworld complicates Hegel’s model by showing that self-consciousness can emerge from within the same system that originally denied it.\n\nBibliography:\n\nAristotle. Topics. C. 330 BCE. Metaphysics. C. 350 BCE. The Basic Works of Aristotle. Ed.\n\nRichard McKeon. New York: Random House, 1941.\n\nAugustine of Hippo, Saint. Confessiones. 398. Trans. F. J. Sheed. The Confessions of St.\n\nAugustine. New York: Sheed & Ward, 1943.\n\nBaars, Bernard J. “The Global Workspace Theory of Consciousness Predictions and Results.”\n\nThe Blackwell Companion to Consciousness. Eds. Susan Schneider and Max Velmans.\n\n2nd edn. Oxford: B. Blackwell, 2017. 229-42\n\nThe Bhagavad Gita: Krishna’s Counsel in Time of War. Trans. Barbara Stoler Miller. New York:\n\nBantam, 1986.\n\nThe Bhagavad Gita. Trans. Shri Purohit Swami. New York: Vintage 1977.\n\nBloom, Paul. “Being in Time: How Much Should We Value the Past, Present, and Future.” New\n\nYorker, 9 July 2021.\n\nChomsky, Noam. Interview in Is the Man Who Is Tall Happy? Michel Gondry. 2013.\n\nDennett, Daniel. Consciousness Explained. Boston: Little, Brown & Co, 1991.\n\nDescartes, René. Meditations. 1641. The Philosophical Writings. 3 vols. Trans. John\n\nCottingham, Robert Stoothoff, and Dugald Murdoch. Cambridge: Cambridge University\n\nPress, 1985.\n\nElias, Norbert. Die höfische Gesellschaft. 1933. Frankfurt: Suhrkamp, 1983. The Court Society.\n\nTrans. Edmund Jephcott. New York: Pantheon, 1969; Dublin: University College Dublin\n\nPress, 2006.\n\n-----------. Die Gesellschaft des Individuen. 1939. Frankfurt: Suhrkamp, 1987. The Society of\n\nIndividuals. Trans. Edmund Jephcott. Oxford: B. Blackwell, 1991.\n\n-----------, with Dunning, Eric. The Quest for Excitement: Sport and Leisure in the Civilizing\n\nProcess. Oxford: Blackwell, 1986.\n\nFanon, Frantz. Peau noire, masques blancs. Paris: Seuil, 1952. Trans. Charles Lam Markmann.\n\nBlack Skin, White Masks. New York: Grove, 1967.\n\n-----------. Les Damnés de la terre. Paris: F. Maspero, 1961. Trans. Constance Farrington. The\n\nWretched of The Earth. Preface by Jean-Paul Sartre. New York: Grove, 1963.\n\nGell, Alfred. Art and Agency: An Anthropological Theory. Oxford: Clarendon Press; New York:\n\nOxford University Press, 1989.\n\nHalligan, Peter & Oakley, David A. \"What If Consciousness Is Just a Product of Our Non-\n\nConscious brain?” The Conversation, 20 December 2018.\n\nHegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich. Phänomenologie des Geistes. Bamberg and Würzburg: J. A.\n\nGoebhardt, 1807. Trans. Terry Pinkard. The Phenomenology of Spirit. Cambridge:\n\nCambridge University Press, 2019. Trans. A.V. Miller. Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit.\n\nOxford: Oxford Univiersity Press, 1976.\n\nHorn, Tina. “What the Rape Scenes in Westworld Say about Rape Culture.” Refinery 29, 4\n\nDecember 2016.\n\nHuxley, Thomas H. “On the Hypothesis That Animals Are Automata, and Its History.”\n\nFortnightly Review 16 (1874): 555-80.\n\nIacoboni, Marco. Mirroring People: The New Science of How We Connect with Others. New\n\nYork: Farrar, Strauss, and Giroux, 2008.\n\nJackson, Frank. “Epiphenomenal Qualia.” Philosophical Quarterly 32 (1982): 127–36.\n\nJaynes, Julian. The Origin of Consciousness in the Breakdown of the Bicameral Mind. Boston:\n\nHoughton Mifflin, 1976.\n\nKalfrin, Valerie. “Westworld and the Power of Repetition.” Final Draft, 2 December 2016.\n\nKant, Immanuel. “Conjectural Beginning of Human History.” 1786. Toward Perpetual Peace\n\nand Other Writings. Ed. Pauline Kleingeld. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2006.\n\nLarmore, Charles. Les Pratiques du moi. Paris: PUF, 2004. Trans. Sharon Bowman. The\n\nPractices of the Self. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 2010.\n\nLemoine, Blake. “Is LaMDA Sentient? An Interview,” The Medium, 11 June 2022.\n\nLibet, B., Gleason, C. A.. Wright, E. W., & Pearl, D. K. “Time of Conscious Intention To Act in\n\nRelation to Onset of Cerebral Activities (Readiness-Potential); The Unconscious\n\nInitiation of a Freely Voluntary Act.” Brain 106 (1983): 623-42.\n\nLucretius, Titus Carus. De rerum matura. 54 BCE. Trans. William Henry Denham Rouse. On the\n\nNature of Things. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924.\n\nManassi, Mauro, and Whitney, David. “Illusion of visual stability through active perceptual\n\nserial dependence.” Science Advances 8: 2, 12 January 2022.\n\nMilton, John. Paradise Lost. 1667. Ed. Gordon Teskey. New York, W. W. Norton, 2005.\n\nMontaigne, Michel de. Essays. 1580/1588/1592. Trans. Donald Frame. The Complete Works of\n\nMontaigne. 1943. Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1958.\n\nNagel, Thomas, “What Is It Like To Be a Bat?” The Philosophical Review 83: 4 (1974), 435-50.\n\nNiedenthal, P.M., Brauer, M., Halberstadt, J.B., & Innes-Ker, A.H. “When Did Her Smile Drop?\n\nFacial Mimicry and the Influences of Emotional State on the Detection of Change in\n\nEmotional Expression.” Cognition and Emotion 15 (2001): 853-64.\n\nNietzsche, Friedrich. Beyond Good and Evil. New York: Vintage, 1984.\n\nNolan, Christopher, dir., and Nolan, Jonathan, screenplay. Memento. Produced by Suzanne Todd\n\nand Jennifer Todd. Summit entertainment, 2000.\n\nParfit, Derek. Reasons and Persons. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1986.\n\nPlato. Republic. C. 380 BCE. Trans. C. D. C. Reeve. Indianapolis, IN: Hackett, 2004.\n\n-----------. Phaedo. C. 347 BCE. Tran. G. M. A. Grube. 2nd edn. Indianapolis, IN: Hackett, 1977.\n\nPlutarchus, Lucius Mestrius. Moralia. 95. Trans. Donald Russell. Selected Essays and\n\nDialogues. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1993.\n\nPorter, Jon. “Google suspends engineer who claims its AI is sentient.” The Verge, 13 June 2022.\n\nRamachandran, V.S. The Tell-Tale Brain: A Neuroscientist’s Quest for What Makes Us Human.\n\nNew York: W.W. Norton, 2011.\n\nReid, Thomas. The Works of Thomas Reid, D.D.: Now Fully Collected, with Selections from His\n\nUnpublished Letters. Ed. T. Reid, W. Hamilton, and D. Stewart. Edinburgh: Maclachlan\n\nand Stewart, 1846.\n\nRiesman, Abraham. \"Westworld Creators Reveal Whether Maeve Is in Control When She Gets\n\nOff the Train.\" Vulture, 6 December 2016.\n\nRothman, Joshua. “Are We Already Living in Virtual Reality?” The New Yorker. April 2, 2018.\n\nScarry, Elaine. The Body in Pain: the Making and Unmaking of the World. Oxford: Oxford\n\nUniversity Press, 1985.\n\nSchopenhauer, Arthur. “Preisschrift über die Freiheit des Willens.” 1839. Trans. Eric F. J. Payne.\n\nPrize Essay on the Freedom of the Will. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1999.\n\nSchurger, Aaron. “The Neuropsychology of Conscious Volition.” The Blackwell Companion to\n\nConsciousness. Eds. Susan Schneider and Max Velmans. 2nd edn. Oxford: B. Blackwell,\n\n2017. 695-710.\n\nScreech, M[ichael] A., ed. Montaigne's Annotated Copy of Lucretius: A Transcription and Study\n\nof the Manuscript, Notes and Pen-Marks. Geneva: Droz, 1998.\n\nSeligman, Adam et al. Ritual and Its Consequences: An Essay on the Limits of Sincerity. New\n\nYork: Oxford University Press, 2008.\n\nSeth, Anil. “Your Brain Hallucinates Your Conscious Reality.” TED Talks. April 2017.\n\nSouth, James B. & Engels, Kimberly S., eds. Westworld and Philosophy. Oxford: Wiley, 2018.\n\nSpecter, Michael. “Partial Recall: Can Neuroscience Help Us Rewrite Our Most Traumatic\n\nMemories?” The New Yorker. May 19, 2014.\n\nSpinoza, Baruch. Ethica Ordine Geometrico Demonstrata. 1665. Opera Posthuma. Amsterdam:\n\nJan Rieuwertsz, 1677. Trans. Samuel Shirley. Ethics, Treatise on the Emendation of the\n\nIntellect. Indianapolis, IN: Hackett, 1992.\n\nVelmans, Max. “Is Human Information Processing Conscious?” Behavioral and Brain Sciences\n\n14: 4 (1991): 651–68.\n\n-----------. “How Could Conscious Experiences Affect Brains?” Journal of Consciousness\n\nStudies 9: 11 (2002): 3-29.\n\nWestworld. Season one, “The Maze.” October 2-December 4, 2016. Season two, “The Door.”\n\nApril 22-June 24, 2018. Created by Jonathan Nolan and Lisa Joy. Produced by Jonathan\n\nNolan, Lisa Joy, J. J. Abrams, Jerry Weintraub, and Bryan Burk. HBO.\n\nWittgenstein, Ludwig. Philosophical Investigations. 1953. Trans. G. E. M. Anscombe. Oxford:\n\nBlackwell, 1958.", "preview": "Westworld follows a futuristic theme park populated by lifelike artificial beings called Hosts, whose memories and behavior are repeatedly controlled by the humans who created them. Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit examines how sel", "buildNumber": 2}, {"id": 8, "title": "Elisa Journal", "category": "Journal", "content": "Personal journal\n\nHow I used to understand consciousness\n\nI used to think of consciousness as something people possessed and utilized at different capacities depending on the situation. Being aware, in any sense of the word, of one’s existence in the world.\n\nHow I understand freedom\n\nI think of freedom as the ultimate goal for a semi-conscious being. Being free is to rid oneself of a prior obstacle or limitation.\n\nWrite about lecture/reading → personal response\n\nSection of team reading that i need to dig deeper into\n\nI really enjoyed Descartes’s meditations as I resonated with his ideas on how consciousness is free and accidental to some extent. His thought process of eliminating previous beliefs in order to construct a fully acceptable response to “what is consciousness” was very interesting. It made me realize that while encounters with mistakes and falsehoods are inevitable, it is only through breaking down the very foundation of our understanding of the mind, that one can reach their personal truths regarding consciousness. Coming into the reading, I wasn't sure what dualism was as a concept. When I was attributed the identity of a “dualist” by professor Hoffman, I was indeed confused. It was uncomfortable to be labeled as something I did not understand yet.\n\nI don’t think we’re the same person throughout our whole existence. If we live in a world that is constantly changing, it would be illogical to think that we don’t change along with it. People will never treat\n\n“ In that experience it has been quite undone, has trembled in every fiber of its being, and everything solid and stable has been shaken to its foundations. Yet, this pure universal movement-the absolute melting away of everything stable-proves to be the simple, essential nature of self-consciousness itself, absolute negativity, pure being-for-self [...]”\n\nI initially had a hard time differentiating consciousness from the self, and from the mind. I used to use those words interchangeably, thus attributing the development of a self and of a mind to the provocation of fear and acts of labor from the slave. I found Hegel incredibly confusing during the first reading, and interpreted his argument as “the fear of death overtakes the slave’s entire being, this fear now establishes his new sense of self”. Now I see it more as a catalytic factor that led to the slave’s initial stage of consciousness, allowing to focus his entire existence on the premise of existing FOR the self. This is rather different from attributing any consciousness to a sense of self, which is what I previously held to believe. I now know that individuality, identity, sense of self, and soul are all different from each other. I also used to believe that the repetition of labor wasn’t something relevant to the development of consciousness, but now know the studied effects of automation and practice in developing autonomy and power, enhancing our impressions of intentionality.\n\nHow Scarry can go in conversation with Hegel\n\nScarry’s idea of projecting pain through creation is an extension of Hegel’s argument regarding work and creation. Hegel attributes the slave’s consciousness to his ability to create something tangible through his work. The materialization of his labor gives his existence purpose, and the intentionality behind his work is a testament to his “being for the self”. Scarry explores this idea with a new depth. She highlights the projection of sentience onto the objects of our creation as a way to communicate our own suffering. As a result, the inanimate and unfeeling retain a part of our sentience and develop a consciousness of their own.\n\nScarry argues that the main reason we project our sentience onto objects is because we want to outwardly show our aversiveness to sentience in unfeeling subjects. We create and imagine things in order to accommodate ourselves, and it all starts from the concept of pain.\n\nFor my build 3, I’ve chosen the concept of reflexivity – the awareness that one is aware of something, different from normal awareness of one's environment. Generally, this occurs during moments of stress, crisis, or perceived threat – anytime in which a non-impulsive decision needs to be reached. I chose this concept because it was the one that appealed most to me when thinking about Hegel’s ideas and seeing Professor Hoffman’s comments on our previous builds. I used it to distinguish consciousness from self-consciousness and how that applied to interdependence between people. I wanted to explore how this awareness was in fact a new layer of consciousness that needs to be worked on to achieve, which I thought applied very well to the concept of labor in the master-slave dialectic. I also contemplated relating Hegel to Scarry’s idea of projection, but while somewhat relevant, I thought it deviated too much from Hegel’s initial theory. While I think the theme of reflexivity is very prominent in the reading and I was able to identify how this was portrayed, I fear it might be hard for me to dig even deeper into this concept, given it is so “out there” already. Perhaps I just need to use this lens on even more passages from the reading, or find a way to interpret it in a new way.\n\nWittgenstein would probably see consciousness as the main differentia within the species. All consciousness is different, or differently perceived - evidenced by the different interpretations and definitions of consciousness there have been. Consciousness itself would be a result of interactions and inward reflection, not a single “thing”. This would in turn discredit the idea that the mind is a centralized system in charge of all our actions and thoughts.\n\nFreedom, according to Wittgenstein, might similarly be a network of small “liberties”. Social freedom, economic freedom, freedom from labor or bondage. They’re all conceptually different, but to be truly free would mean to be untied to any constraint. The definition of freedom, would therefore be a result of our actions, circumstances, and mindset. It can be individual, institutional, momentary, chronic…etc. The nature of definitions of freedom varies contextually, yet all these definitions will be valid.\n\nWittgenstein would consider a self the amalgamation of everything that we define as self-consciousness. That would include our awareness of projection onto others, our values and thoughts from religion, our interactions with others. There is little commonality among any of these definitions from our readings, but the union of all of these strengthen the idea that all of these represent the “self”.\n\nUnfortunately we have not been able to get the agent to change our website. Despite it replying with “Done”, no change has been made. Obviously it’s a bit frustrating as we’re trying to figure out where the issue is coming from. We will, however, contact Johnathon Beals to try to fix it.\n\nSome of the themes we’ve explored over the past few weeks, both in Hegel’s writing and in general discussion can be tied to the show Westworld as a whole. As mentioned in class, the camera angles and set design are very intentional in portraying themes of entrapment, hierarchy, relationships…etc. A specific example that comes to mind is the costume design in Westworld. The sterile/fitted-looking clothing of the engineers and corporate people vs the old-timey, western appearance of the hosts. Beyond the literal meaning of this contrast, the visual contrast also separates the “self-conscious” from the unaware.\n\nI’ve given agency to clocks and watches. I give them a certain responsibility to “work” at all times. I still use it.\n\nDiscussion about Eve being a rational figure. Often portrayed as the weaker individual who succumbs to Satan’s tricks. Yet, Milton portrays her as an incredibly rational person who deliberates and considers consequences. Despite her making the wrong decision, the feeling that ensues is not regret (which is a passive reaction), but acceptance and recalibration based on the new circumstances. This redefined the term “freedom” for me.\n\nSince the start of college I have been in a constant battle with myself. I cannot figure out how to balance academic stress with the desire to live happily in a new environment. My “master-self” (the self that wants me to work tirelessly to achieve good grades, transfer to Ross, get an internship…etc)  is controlling my “slave-self” (the one actually doing everything) to a point where I’m starting to feel burnout (the upheaval of the slave). I find solace, however, in the fact that this will potentially lead to a future sense of relief.\n\nI think coming to a new country changed the way I interact and view myself. I learned to practice my language (not in fluency but in social interactions), my mannerisms, my tone and volume. The friendships I have now are so dissimilar to ones I’ve had in the past and it is because I am a different “self” from my past “self”. I had always thought of myself as an outgoing, carefree person. However now, inspecting my priorities, my habits and routines, that has changed, and so has my view of myself.", "preview": "How I used to understand consciousness I used to think of consciousness as something people possessed and utilized at different capacities depending on the situation. Being aware, in any sense of the word, of one’s existence in th"}, {"id": 9, "title": "Jimena Build 1", "category": "Build", "content": "Jimena Sanchez Aguirre: Build 1 - 9/11/2026\n\nChosen Passage: (§195:) Although the fear of the master is the beginning of wisdom, consciousness is not therein aware of being-for-self. Through work, however, the servant becomes conscious of what he truly is. [... The master's] desire has reserved to itself the pure negating of the object and thereby an unalloyed feeling of self. But that is the reason why this satisfaction is itself only a fleeting one, for it lacks objectivity and permanence. Work, on the other hand, requires desire held in check, fleetingness staved off; in other words, work forms and shapes the thing. [...] This negative middle term or formative activity is at the same time the individuality or pure being-for-self of consciousness which now, in the work outside of it, acquires an element of permanence. It is in this way, therefore, that consciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence. (Hegel, 195)\n\nA carpenter can spend hours shaping a piece of wood into a specific form. But as the wood changes, so does the carpenter’s understanding of how to work with it. His labor changes the object, but the process also changes him, teaching him how to understand and interact with something outside himself. This raises a much older philosophical question: can the conditions we live in cause consciousness to develop, or can they prevent it from doing so?\n\nHegel explores this very question in The Phenomenology of Spirit, tracing the development of self-consciousness as it changes through a person’s activity and encounters with the world around them. For Hegel, consciousness does not exist as a fixed awareness that a person possesses; it develops as a person acts and comes to recognize themselves through those actions. This assumes that consciousness does not separate itself from our actions and circumstances, but instead persists as something that can be changed through our engagement with the world. What a person recognizes about themselves depends, in part, on the situation in which they live in and, more importantly, on what that situation requires them to do.\n\nFurthermore, Hegel introduces the master-servant relationship, in which their different situations create different ways of acting upon the world, and those actions ultimately lead their self-consciousness in opposite directions.\n\nThe servant enters the master-servant relationship without full awareness of this independence. Although the servant possesses self-consciousness, it  resembles something enclosed in a shell, unable to recognize its own independence. His fear and vulnerability force him to suppress his immediate desires and work according to the master’s demands. Hegel describes him as “not therein aware of being-for-self” (195). His problem lies not in his lack of  consciousness, but in his failure to recognize himself as independent; his understanding of himself still relies on his position under the master. Because he restrains his desires, the servant must work upon the world rather than simply consume it. Hegel’s claim that work “forms and shapes the thing” (195) therefore applies to the servant as well: in shaping something outside himself, he begins to recognize his own ability to shape the world.\n\nHegel describes this realization when he writes that the worker “comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence.” (195) The object becomes important because, through changing it, the servant can see the capability  of  his own actions. The servant does not become independent simply because his circumstances improve; they do not. Yet this complicates the idea that developing consciousness simply means becoming free, since the servant’s ability to recognize his independence emerges from a situation in which he remains unfree. Rather, the very conditions that restrict him force him to develop the ability to recognize himself through sustained activity in the world. His situation therefore changes his self-consciousness because, through his labor, he comes to recognize his own independence in what he has produced.\n\nHowever, Hegel’s argument also suggests that a situation can hinder the development of consciousness rather than advance it. The servant's position forces him into sustained labor, while the master's position allows him to avoid it. Hegel calls this an “unalloyed feeling of self,” but this satisfaction lasts  “only as a fleeting one” because the master’s actions leave nothing permanent through which he can recognize his own activity.\n\nThe master’s situation therefore undermines the very independence he tries to establish. He can satisfy his desires immediately, while the servant persistently performs the labor that makes this possible. This creates a contradiction in the master’s consciousness: although he appears independent, his position makes him dependent on the servant’s work and recognition. Hegel’s description of the master’s satisfaction as “only a fleeting one” (195) shows why this dependence matters. By avoiding the restraint and sustained activity that shape the servant’s consciousness, the master has little opportunity to recognize his own independence through his actions. Instead of encountering the resistance and permanence that could deepen his self-understanding, he repeatedly receives immediate satisfaction without confronting the activity that produces it.\n\nThrough the contrast between the master and servant, Hegel reveals that consciousness is shaped not simply by the conditions we occupy, but by what those conditions require us to do. Complicating the assumption that consciousness develops simply according to a person’s position. But if consciousness develops through our actions and interactions, how much of what we call “self” can truly stand as our own? Hegel suggests that consciousness can change how a person acts upon the world, but this leaves open a larger question: does consciousness itself create a new causal chain in the physical world, or do the conditions that produce our consciousness ultimately determine our every action??\n\nEliminate the conclusion that AI produced: instead, identify the underlying assumptions your author makes about consciousness.\n\nIdentifying the assumptions your author is making about consciousness will allow your essay to remain open-ended, instead of pretending to advance a definitive argument about consciousness\n\nend by explaining how the question of whether (and how) consciousness can create a new causal chain in the physical world remains a problem.\n\nyou will want to finish it with open-ended questions instead of a conclusion\n\nBetter results come from submitting your draft to AI and asking it: “Where would a reader lose interest or get confused? Give me specifics on what to improve, don’t focus on the format: [copy your revised draft here].”\n\nReferences: indicate page numbers within parentheses: \"... the theory of mind\" (45). Add the author's last name (Jaynes, 272) only if it is not already clear. For Westworld simply use the season/episode numbers: (1.4). Do not worry about including a bibliography; that will be provided to you later.\n\nGuide your instructor's reading of your essay by using the comment button to the far right at the top to explain the ways you took peer feedback into account.\n\nyour grade will reflect 1) how much you reworked AI-sourced raw material to make it your own, in keeping with \"Ford's rubric\" below; and 2) how much you were able to understand in the reading.", "preview": "Chosen Passage: (§195:) Although the fear of the master is the beginning of wisdom, consciousness is not therein aware of being-for-self. Through work, however, the servant becomes conscious of what he truly is. [... The master's]", "buildNumber": 1}, {"id": 10, "title": "Anthony Build 1", "category": "Build", "content": "Chosen Passage (Hegel, §§186–187)\n\n§186: “Self-consciousness is, to begin with, simple being-for-self, self-equal through excluding from itself everything else. For it, its essence and absolute object is ‘I’; and in this immediacy, or in this mere being, of its being-for-self, it is an individual. What is ‘other’ for it is an unessential, negatively characterized object. But the ‘other’ is also a self-consciousness; one individual is confronted by another individual […] Each is indeed certain of its own self, but not of the other, and therefore its own self-certainty still has no truth. For […] according to the concept of recognition this is possible only when each is for the other what the other is for it, only when each, in its own self through its own action and again through the action of the other, achieves this pure abstraction of being-for-self.”\n\n§187: “Insofar as it is what is done by the other, each thus aims at the death of the other. But in doing so, what is done by way of oneself is also involved, for the former involves putting one’s own life on the line. Thus the relation of both self-consciousnesses means that only through a life-and-death struggle can each prove its worth to itself, and that both prove their worth to each other. They must engage in this struggle, for each must elevate the self-certainty of existing for themselves to truth, both in the case of the other and in their own case. And it is solely by staking one’s life that freedom is won, namely, that as a result the essential nature of self-consciousness is proven to be not just being, not in the immediate way self-consciousness emerges, not in being absorbed within the expanse of life, but rather, that there is nothing on hand in itself which could not be a vanishing moment for it, that is, that self-consciousness is only pure being-for-itself. The individual who has not risked their life may admittedly be recognized as a person, but they have not attained the truth of being recognized as an independent self-consciousness.”\n\nBuild 1\n\nThe moment before raising a hand. A thought that sounded complete in my head suddenly feels unfinished when I imagine thirty other people hearing it. I become aware not only of what I think, but also of how I might appear through it. Is this discomfort the beginning of self-consciousness, or am I constructing a version of myself for an audience? Hegel suggests that consciousness can remain certain by separating itself from everything else. Self-consciousness must risk that certainty before someone who can answer back. This raises a new question. Why should another person’s response have the power to make my identity true?\n\nPrivate certainty is easy to maintain because nothing outside the self has tested it. The self therefore wants another consciousness to test its claim to independence and still accept that claim. Success would show that its identity matters beyond its own mind. It would also protect the self from the fear that its certainty is only self-deception. This test has value only when the other person is free to refuse. Forced recognition proves nothing. Yet each consciousness initially treats the other as an object. Each wants an independent judge but refuses to grant the other real independence. This is why each is “certain of its own self, but not of the other,” and why its “self-certainty still has no truth” (3). Hegel’s grammar shows that this problem cannot be solved by either self alone.\n\nThe balanced clause “through its own action and again through the action of the other” gives both consciousnesses a role in producing recognition (3). The sentence moves back and forth between them. Neither has full control. At this point, I become less interested in whether recognition succeeds than in what the other person is supposed to recognize. We never experience another person’s consciousness directly. We interpret speech, expressions, and actions as signs of an inner self. If both people arrange these signs for an audience, recognition may not reveal stable selves. It may be a social performance that helps create the appearance of them.\n\nThis possibility leads to another problem. If recognition helps construct the self, can that self still be independent? Hegel says recognition occurs when “each is for the other what the other is for it” (3). A generous reading suggests that independence does not require isolation. Each person can remain free by taking part equally in the exchange. Still, this freedom depends on a relationship that neither person controls. The self may be free from domination without ever being free from others. Freedom here may mean not freedom from influence, but freedom within mutual influence. That changes Hegel’s idea of independence. The self proves independence not by existing before relationships, but by surviving the uncertainty those relationships introduce. Still, if others help produce its identity, it remains unclear what belongs to the self alone.\n\nThis uncertainty becomes physical in the “life-and-death struggle” (3). Each consciousness tries to prove its freedom by risking its life before the other. The struggle seems to promise victory. One self could defeat the other and force its claim to independence upon them. Yet such a victory would defeat its own purpose. If the other is destroyed or completely controlled, their judgment is no longer independent. Their recognition would then prove nothing. Victory in the struggle would become failure in recognition. The surviving consciousness would return to the same private certainty from which the conflict began.\n\nBecause victory cannot secure true recognition, the self remains dependent on a response it cannot control. That response matters because it comes from someone independent of my wishes. Yet for it to matter, I must also believe that I am encountering another consciousness. I cannot experience that consciousness directly. I can only interpret its words, expressions, and actions. This creates a deeper problem for Hegel. Recognition requires contact with an independent mind, but the existence of that contact can never be fully confirmed. When I acknowledge another person, am I truly recognizing their consciousness, or am I assigning consciousness to the behavior I observe? If both people rely on this same assumption, does their recognition produce truth, or does it merely sustain the aforementioned social performance? Hegel shows that another person’s response can shape the self, but he leaves open how an uncertain encounter between minds can create independent action in the physical world.", "preview": "§186: “Self-consciousness is, to begin with, simple being-for-self, self-equal through excluding from itself everything else. For it, its essence and absolute object is ‘I’; and in this immediacy, or in this mere being, of its bei", "buildNumber": 1}, {"id": 11, "title": "Ryan Journal", "category": "Journal", "content": "Honors 241 Personal Journal\n\n9/9/26 Disc.\n\nHow I Used to Understand Consciousness: I used to think consciousness was simply being aware of yourself and your surroundings. I didn’t think there was any conflict between consciousness and its surroundings or other external factors. Just a mind that knows it is a knowing entity.\n\nI understand freedom in the sense that while our own biology and other factors encourage us to make certain decisions, it is ultimately our own conscious choice to choose to commit certain acts, think certain thoughts, and believe in certain things.\n\n9/16/26 Disc.\n\nAn experience I can relate to reading is always questioning things in school and whether or not the system itself is trying to make me perceive something a certain way, like in history class, for example. This parallels the evil demon concept from Descartes.\n\nSomething I would like to understand more from the lecture is maybe how Descartes interprets mathematical concepts as being real, and not just something an evil demon put into his head.\n\n9/23/26 Lect.\n\n1: Only once I started hitting the gym consistently did I start to better understand how hard work pays off over time, little by little. It gave me a new appreciation for my own physical health and got me addicted to achieving hard goals. I learned a lot about my body, nutrition, and the movements. Before this, I wasn’t truly concerned with any of this and was significantly more lazy and idle. The gym made me happier, more confident, and altered me in many ways. I even made friends through this hobby, and it altered my path in life.\n\n2: I think discipline relates much more to my experience, mainly because that is the pathway in which many of my habits, thoughts, and ideas have developed. I am still rooted in reality, but when faced with options, instead of detaching, I choose a more predisposed path. I stick more to rules and regimen. I have a lot of habits, and my behavior is somewhat fixed, in my opinion.\n\n9/23/26 Disc.\n\nReflecting on the lecture, I thought about the entire phenomenon of how even the choice to step back and question reality and free will may even be programmed into certain people. What I mean is, I thought it was interesting how this, in itself, may not be a real choice but rather something that we may think is a choice or an enlightened decision, but is, in fact, the opposite.\n\nIn subsection 190 of the Hegel reading, something I kinda understand differently now is that the idea that the master servant relationship ends with the servant achieving a more independent and enlightened sense of self may be somewhat flawed in reasoning when combined with other ideas of consciousness. This sense of independence may not be a response to the dynamic but could simply be predetermined in the brain, as was discussed in lecture.\n\n9/28/26 Disc.\n\nI think Scarry connects to Hegel by describing how humans have needs, and through those needs, they create things which then exceed their expectations and help them develop. Scarry describes this using objects, while Hegel seems to do this using the master servant idea. Humans have apparent desires, and these desires result in a conflict; the result of that conflict, while providing an expected outcome, also helps them- more specifically, the servant- to develop further in unexpected ways.\n\n9/30/26 Lect.\n\nScarry talks about how people, in order to truly empathize with other individuals, have to first empathize with inanimate objects. Among these ideas, there are stages in which this happens. Pain, projection, animation, identification, and empathization. First, you feel an indescribable pain, which you then project onto something else. You then find comfort in that thing, start to identify with it, and then finally you feel empathy for it.\n\n9/30/26 Disc.\n\nFor build 3, I chose reflexivity because Hegel talks a lot about how people’s consciousnesses develop through the interactions between each other. Specifically, Hegel talks about a master servant diuretic, and a battle between them that gives them their roles. In the battle, one person has to be completely willing to die; that person usually wins. Reflexivity is the quality of when a person recognizes their consciousness is in a different state than the usual in one's environment, and I think this is achieved during this battle, and in the servant afterward. I did not really have any questions or have trouble doing this.\n\n10/7/26 Lect.\n\nI think Wittgenstein would describe consciousness as a collective knowledge of different things rather than just one thing. It's a combination of a plethora of knowledge and connections.\n\nI think Wittgenstein would define freedom as an illusion. One might believe they have the freedom to make decisions and even be aware of their choices, but in reality, this is false. Social norms largely influence and determine the choices of people.\n\nWittgenstein would say that the sense of self is an overlap of many different qualities, as well as a culmination of a majority of thoughts, ideas, and relationships.\n\n10/7/26 Disc.\n\nOur website does not work. Our agent has yet to perform any of our demands.\n\n10/12/26 Disc.\n\nMy writing as of now has explored the relationship between reflexivity and Hegelian ideas. In Westworld, the hosts become somewhat reflexive, understanding that they are entities with awareness; however, the level of awareness is quite ambiguous. Towards the end, it's clear that Maeve and Dolores reach this reflexivity, but along the way, it's unclear. Maeve thinks she is fully conscious only to realize that something is giving her thoughts milliseconds before she “comes up” with them. This follows reflexivity being a spectrum in that she is partially reflexive but not entirely autonomous.\n\n10/28/26 Disc.\n\nIn lecture today, I thought it was very interesting when Professor Hoffman discussed the different levels of free choice. It really resonated with me- the 4 levels- and, related to those, having made a choice on every level. I never truly understood this in an organized way until today, when he explained it.\n\n11/4/26 Lect.\n\nInternally, I have been struggling with wanting to work extremely hard, to put progress over experience, and with wanting to experience life to the fullest without worrying about the “rat race.” This has been extremely hard. Sometimes I want to go hang out with friends, go to parties, travel, all these things. Sometimes I want to say, “away with this,” and go extremely hard at academics, the gym, career aspirations, things like this. I believe I am the slave to the external world in this way. I’m letting all these things control how I feel and how I think about myself. However, I know that if I stop letting these things control me, I would be able to create a better balance. Instead of trying to live up to a label like “hard worker,” I would feel happier and fulfilled.\n\n11/9/26 Lect.\n\nOne sustained undertaking was probably high school. Starting out, I didn’t view myself as very smart or talented, but as I continued through high school and took on new challenges, I discovered that I have a lot of talent and am a lot smarter than I originally thought. I have a lot more to offer and am more capable than when I first began.", "preview": "9/9/26 Disc. How I Used to Understand Consciousness: I used to think consciousness was simply being aware of yourself and your surroundings. I didn’t think there was any conflict between consciousness and its surroundings or other"}, {"id": 12, "title": "Jimena Journal", "category": "Journal", "content": "How you used to understand consciousness\n\nI used to understand consciousness as something that is managed by the brain. I believed consciousness was something that is subconsciously controlled by the brain and that we have some sort of control over what our consciousness wants and decides.\n\nHow you understand freedom.\n\nI think freedom is the ability to have control over something, not being dictated by someone else\n\nParagraph about lecture this week (opinion or personal experience that you can correlate to a lecture this week)\n\nI found the lectures this week to be confusing, but at the same time I understand where Descartes comes from. One cannot be sure of everything that is happening around the outside world, so one must question it before assuming it is true. Although even on the inside one can doubt who they are, they still know they are a thinking being.\n\nQuestion on team reading\n\nI am a bit confused about the light metaphor that Hegel uses. Although I understand its basics of how the light doesn’t know that the room is unlit because everywhere he looks the room is “lit” (because of his lamp), I am confused as to how that applies to concepts Hegel later goes on to explain in the reading.\n\nWhen in your life intentional, repetitive behavior changed you and how it did so not only by training you to acquire a new physical skill, but, also, by modifying your attitudes, outlook, or even personality.\n\nSomething I have recently taken up on is going on walks, as I have come to repeat it more and more, not only have I found myself to have a higher endurance to walking long distances, but I have also come to appreciate the scenery around me.\n\nHow does Detachment or discipline  (models for achieving consciousness) relate to your experience. Is one more applicable than the other?\n\nI think it is important to maintain discipline in order to work towards achieving consciousness. Discipline helps you stay more focused towards your goal and ignore the surrounding “noise” around you. I feel like detachment is a much harder method for achieving consciousness, I get attached to things very easily and even though it might help achieve consciousness, it might not help.\n\nAre we the same person throughout our lives?\n\nI don't think we remain the same person throughout our lifespan. Although we remain in the same body, our consciousness evolves through experiences we have, our cells also regenerate which create different versions of us compared to when we were kids. As one experiences so many things throughout our lifetimes, those experiences come to change how we act and who we are as a person.\n\nWhat part of the passage that you chose for Build 1 do you understand differently now?\n\nA part of my passage that I now view in a different light is how Hegel connects to Socrates. They both argue that reasoning and reality changes one’s perspective and consciousness. The worker is forced to live in this fake reality where he believes that he is dependent on the master (kind of like living in a cave) as he knows no other reality than this. However, through work he “emerges out of the cave” and comes to learn about this new reality that he didn’t know where the master is dependent on the servant, which eventually changes his self-consciousness and evolves it.\n\nRelate Hegel to Scarry\n\nScarry’s reading can connect to Hegel. Scary talks about how objects are reflections of human emotions and desires, and how we reflect on things which eventually develop our self consciousness. Similarly, Hegel also talks about reflection and how by talking to people we reflect on each other and develop and increase our self-consciousness.\n\nScarry’s phenomenal account of consciousness summary\n\nObjects are reflections of people’s desires, and thoughts\n\nThrough the reflection of these objects one becomes more self conscious\n\nHumans often tend to blame objects for things that have happened to them, but aren't the humans the ones that made those objects?\n\nEmpathy and reflection of objects is achieved by…. How can you and another person experience the same pain? And have the same empathy?\n\nBuild 3 progress\n\nOn my build 3, I chose to focus on reconsolidation as my consciousness quality. I found it easy to focus on the past and future, but did struggle on how to relate in the present. I feel like I need to be more analytical in my essay, since I feel like I’m mostly summarizing. I will focus my build 3 in more analytical and philosophical concepts, I also need to change the intro and ending.\n\nHow you think Wittgenstein might describe consciousness\n\nI think Wittgenstein might describe consciousness as a network of thoughts and experiences, not defining consciousness as one single thing, but kind of as a category where the things inside consciousness have something shared.\n\nWhat would Wittgenstein say about “freedom”?\n\nI think Wittgenstein would say that one might not truly be able to define freedom, the cluster of ideas in the network of consciousness contain different ideas of freedom\n\nWhat Wittgenstein might say about your “self”\n\nWittgenstein would say that your self is composed of many aspects that together connect in one way or another and make up the category of “self”\n\nWebsite thoughts and feedback:\n\nAlthough we have used the comment feature and agent, our website has not been updated. But we have been planning what we are going to add to the website.\n\nHow does my build connect with Westword?\n\nMy build connects with Westworld through Reconsolidation. Although Maeve changes and evolves her thinking and her view of self, she remains the same person throughout. Through this change, Maeve takes her evolution by selecting and reinterpreting past experiences, present circumstances, and future possibilities as one’s identity changes\n\nSomething I’ve given agency a lot of times is my alarm clock. Whenever the alarm clock doesn’t go off, you tend to blame the alarm for now waking you up\n\nFrom today’s lecture…\n\nSomething that resonated with me from today’s lecture was seeing how reconsolidation can be applied into Adam and Eve’s relationship, this is particularly interesting as I have been writing my essays on reconsolidation so it is very interesting to see how although different texts, they can still carry some sort of same meaning or qualities.\n\nAnother thing I found interesting is thinking about how women are portrayed in Westworld along with other epics and Paradise Lost.\n\nIdentify some internal conflict you have experienced and try mapping it onto an internal master-slave dialect.\n\nWhen I moved to the United States and knew no English, I felt very lonely and suppressed who I was in order to try and fit in with the rest, hiding my culture and what I liked in order to appeal to others. In this dialect, the master is my past self; the one that didn’t have to worry about making friends and the one that roamed without any worries. The slave is my new self who has realized value through hard fork and fear.\n\nIdentify one sustained undertaking in your life that ended up changing your view of who you were.\n\nWhen I was little, I used to compare myself with others all the time, especially after moving to the US. However, one day I met my neighbors who we proud and embraced all parts of their identity, I would look up to them so much and want to be like them; eventually, I reallized that I can be like them and not compare myself wit others?", "preview": "I used to understand consciousness as something that is managed by the brain. I believed consciousness was something that is subconsciously controlled by the brain and that we have some sort of control over what our consciousness "}, {"id": 13, "title": "Evan Journal", "category": "Journal", "content": "Define consciousness and how your understanding has changed:\nI used to understand consciousness as the thing that determined how I live. I thought\nconsciousness was us living in the moment, making our own decisions for ourselves, and being\nable to determine right from wrong. I also thought that we had a free consciousness and that we\ncould do whatever we wanted to.\nDefine freedom:\nFor freedom, I understand freedom as the ability to do whatever you want, whenever you want.\nPeople get jobs so that they can get money so that they can achieve freedom.\nWhen did you use intentional repetition/discipline in your life\nOne of the things that I had to use repetition and intentional behavior in was piano. I was\npreparing a very difficult piece called La Campanella and it took me nearly 2 years of practicing\nalmost every day to achieve. There were many times where I wanted to give up and stop\nplaying and do something else but I was able to perform the piece to a pretty high standard at\nthe end of it all. It taught me to look at life from a more long-term standpoint and also that\nfrustration is all a part of the journey. I think I have become a much more patient and\nunderstanding person because of that.\nWhich definition of freedom relates more to you?\nI think for me discipline relates much more than detachment. Like Maeve, I think a lot of the\nchoices I make are based off what I am attached to, not what i am detached from. A lot of my\nlife also revolve around discipline, such as practicing the piano, doing schoolwork even when I\ndon’t want to, and going to the gym. Because of this, I would have to reject the detachment\nmodel and I would say that my experience is much more applicable to the discipline model.\n\nReflection on lecture: I think the difference between discipline and detachment is very\ninteresting. One one hand, you have to work very hard to attain consciousness and it takes a lot\nof discipline and suffering to truly become free. But on the other hand, people like Jaynes argue\nthat development of consciousness is about stepping back and evaluating your thoughts and\nemotions before you act. I was a little confused about the detachment part of lecture so I’m not\nexactly sure if what I’m saying is right or not.\nWhat do you understand differently about your reading that you did not understand before:\nThe thing I understand differently about the master-slave relationship from today’s lecture is that\nI think the idea of discipline can be applied to this. Before I used to think that the slave was\ndoing work purely out of fear of the master. However, I think the slave uses a lot of discipline\nwhen he is putting in work to try to free himself from the master. This is probably not work that\nhe really wants to be doing but he does it anyway because it is the only way to be free. So after\nall that suffering and work he is able to look back and his consciousness has now developed\nfurther and he sees himself as a true conscious being.\nWhat is a connection between Hegel and Scarry:\n\n\fBoth Scarry and Hegel talk about a shared focus on the relationship between consciousness\nand the external world. Hegel argues that consciousness develops into self-consciousness\nthrough one’s experiences with the outside world. Individuals come to understand themselves\nthrough their relationships with the world and other people. Scarry focuses on how physical pain\nis used to create objects. Hegel describes action and creation as a way for a person to\nexternalize themselves and recognize themselves in the world, but Scarry argues that severe\npain can make this externalization difficult because pain is internal and hard to communicate if\nyou are not experiencing it. Both writers are therefore concerned with how inner experience\nbecomes connected to the outside world, but they emphasize different possibilities.\nWhat confused you about the Scarry reading today:\nOne thing that really confused me about Scarry was the reciprocity of the object. She says that\nwhen we make/put an action onto an object, we are giving the object some of our characteristics\n(like our life being vulnerable and not immortal) but then we get back some of that immortality\nthrough reciprocity which doesn’t make sense to me.\n\nSummary of Scarry: Scarry uses the idea of physical pain to prove that our experiences and\nperception and feeling of “what it is to be me” is not reducible to brain activity. She does this by\nworking through a five step process: pain, projection, animation, identification, and empathy.\nShe argues that we need to treat objects as people before we can treat people as people.\nWhat quality of consciousness did you choose:\nThe thing that I chose to write about was phenomenality. I think this is easy to write about\nbecause it is the easiest to understand out of the 5 in my opinion, as it is simply “what it feels\nlike to be me” . I also feel like it is pretty easy to talk about in the context of Hegel. However, I\nam struggling with fully understanding the actual reading and that is making the writing of it\npretty difficult.\nHow Wittgenstein may describe consciousness:\nI think Wittgenstein would describe consciousness as something that probably cannot be\ndefined. It has a complex network of traits and characteristics but other animals also have the\nsame traits and so there is not one defining trait of consciousness that differentiates it from\nsomething else. While our common sense may say that consciousness has one defining trait,\nthis may not be the case when you actually look at it. For example, if we think about reflexivity\nvs phenominality, there is a lot of difference in those two traits and both can maybe be replicated\nby animals. But it is hard to connect each concept of consciousness to the next. Some people’s\ndefinitions may overlap and some may have nothing to do with each other and both can be\nvalid.\nWhat Wittgenstein would say about freedom:\nSimilar to consciousness, I think Wittgenstein would say that there is no easy way to define\nfreedom. Many different philosophers have different definitions and it is more like a network than\na simple term. This means that some people may agree with others and some people may not\n\n\fagree with those same people and both can have a valid argument. Freedom is also used in\nvarious contexts. For example, the freedom to make your own decisions is different than being\nphysically free and not held captive, so they may have more/less objectivity depending on the\nsituation/context.\nWhat Wittgenstein would say about “self”:\nAgain, like the previous two, Wittgenstein would probably say that the self is not defined by one\ncharacteristic, but the overlap of many different characteristics. Many people may have different\nideas on what the self actually is and how they are different from others. But there may also be\nsome people that share qualities with other people and some that share none. Therefore, this\nsystem of overlapping/not is how we can determine our self and individuality.\nThoughts on website:\nI think we have a decent idea for our website but we are still figuring out how the ai agent works.\nIt is pretty frustrating because I feel like I could do a better job and easier without ai. But we will\ncontinue to add to the planning doc and make it better and better. It will be a longer process\nwhich is fine but we definitely need to figure it out soon.\nHow our individual topic applies to the audience’s cognitive thinking throughout watching\nWestworld:\nHegel’s ideas about continuity and reconsolidation shape how the audience understands identity\nthroughout Westworld. Hegel argues that consciousness must reconsider its sense of self when\nwhat once made that identity feel stable begins to fall apart. The hosts experience this as their\nmemories challenge the realities they have accepted and force them to reconsider who they\nare. Similarly, the audience must revise its understanding of the hosts as new information\nchanges the meaning of their past experiences. This connects to Hegel’s argument that identity\nis not simply preserved but must be reconstructed as consciousness confronts contradictions in\nits existing understanding of itself. As viewers discover that the hosts’ memories and identities\nare more complicated than they initially appeared, they must decide how to interpret what they\npreviously believed. In this way, Westworld encourages the audience to experience a version of\nreconsolidation themselves, questioning whether a stable sense of identity comes from holding\nonto the past or reconsidering what that past means.\n\nWhat is an example of an object that you have transferred some of your emotions into:\nWhen I used to play piano, I was practicing a really difficult piece and it was very frustrating\nbecause some days I would play it really good and other days I would not. I started to notice\nthat when I was frustrated I would unconsciously play louder and faster as if I was trying to\nprove that I could play it as good as the day before.\nWhat is something that resonated with you in lecture today:\n\n\fI think the idea between fatalism and freedom is something that was really interesting to me and\nsomething that I didn’t think about when I first read paradise lost. Satan is stuck on this idea of\nhimself, and he thinks that he possesses this one true self and there is no way for him to\nchange that. His selfhood exists entirely by identifying himself with another. Whereas for Eve,\nfreedom is the ability to be able to change your idea about yourself and understand that there\nmay not be one true self. I think there is a spectrum and most people fall somewhere in the\nmiddle, where they know they have values that they are not willing to change but there are\nmany things about their personality that can change over time.\n\nIdentify some internal conflict you have experienced and try mapping it onto an internal\nmaster-slave dialectic\nWhen I played piano, I used to be able to read the sheet music and I never really had to study\nfor my lessons. But then, the music got a lot more technically difficult, and I had a few bad\nlessons so I had to start playing a lot in order to achieve my goal of being able to play these\nreally difficult pieces up on stage in front of a lot of people. It was very hard, and it required\ndesire held in check as Hegel said because there were many times where I really did not want\nto do the work, but at the end the work paid off and I was able to perform all the pieces that I\nwanted to.\n\nIdentify one sustained undertaking in your life that ended up changing your view of who you\nwere.\n\nOne thing that I started to undertake in my life was I refused to get anything below an A in high\nschool. If I had an A-, I would have anxiety about it until my grade was resolved. It really pissed\nme off whenever I had a missing assignment or I didn’t do as well on a test as I should have.\nWhen I look back on my high school years, I realize how that mindset has built the foundation of\nmyself today. I no longer look at myself as needing to be perfect, but I understand that if you\nstrive for perfection and you don’t reach it, you will still end up in a good spot.\n\n​", "preview": "Define consciousness and how your understanding has changed: I used to understand consciousness as the thing that determined how I live. I thought consciousness was us living in the moment, making our own decisions for o"}, {"id": 14, "title": "Evan Build 1", "category": "Build", "buildNumber": 1, "content": "Selected passage:\n“For this servile consciousness has been fearful, not\nof this or that particular thing, or merely at odd moments, but its\nwhole being has been seized with dread. For it has experienced the\nfear of death—the absolute Master. In that experience it has been\nquite undone, has trembled in every fiber of its being, and everything\nsolid and stable has been shaken to its foundations. Yet, this pure\nuniversal movement—the absolute melting away of everything\nstable—proves to be the simple, essential nature of selfconsciousness itself, absolute negativity, pure being-for-self […]\nFurthermore, the servant’s consciousness does not dissolve\neverything stable merely in principle; in his service he actually brings\nthis about. Through his service he rids himself of his attachment to\nnatural existence in every single detail and gets rid of it by working\non it.\n(§195:) Although the fear of the master is the beginning of\nwisdom, consciousness is not therein aware of being-for-self.\nThrough work, however, the servant becomes conscious of what he\ntruly is. [… The master’s] desire has reserved to itself the pure\nnegating of the object and thereby an unalloyed feeling of self. But\nthat is the reason why this satisfaction is itself only a fleeting one, for\nit lacks objectivity and permanence. Work, on the other hand,\nrequires desire held in check, fleetingness staved off; in other words,\nwork forms and shapes the thing. […] This negative middle term or\nformative activity is at the same time the individuality or pure beingfor-self of consciousness which now, in the work outside of it,\nacquires an element of permanence. It is in this way, therefore, that\nconsciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being\n[of the object] its own independence.\n(§196:) [… Thus the servant] becomes for himself, someone\nexisting on his own account […] Through this rediscovery of himself\nby himself, the servant realizes that it is precisely in his work,\nwherein he seemed to have only an alienated existence, that he\nacquires a mind of his own.”\n\nYou look over the edge of the boat at the murky, ink-stained sea below. Thousands of meters\nfrom land, beneath the fear of falling lies something more unsettling: the awareness that you\ncould die. Fear is a feeling we have all felt, but rarely are we put in a situation where our life is\non the line. But whose awareness is this—a decision your consciousness makes to be afraid, or\na set of physical brain processes that consciousness just reports on afterward? If it is only the\nlatter, the \"you\" that trembles at the rail is not doing anything at all; it is watching itself react. The\nharder question is what happens next: once we become aware of what is happening to us, does\nconsciousness merely experience it, or can that awareness change how we understand\nourselves and what we do? Hegel's account of the servant's fear of death provides a way to\nexplore that question.\nHegel exemplifies this through the servant's experience of \"the fear of death,\" which\nbrings about \"the absolute melting away of everything stable\" (5). This is not dread at \"this or\nthat particular thing,\" but a fear before which his \"whole being\" trembles (5). The servant had\nidentified himself with his immediate, given existence: his fixed place in the world, his status as\na thing that simply is what it is. Death threatens that “natural existence” (5), exposing that he\nhad mistaken it for something fixed and permanent. Importantly, this realization happens to the\nservant; he does not choose to feel his \"whole being… seized with dread\" (5). If he is simply\nreacting to fear, the transformation may be something that happens to consciousness rather\nthan something consciousness effects. But Hegel insists this dissolution is also \"the simple,\nessential nature of self-consciousness itself\" (5): consciousness is forced to confront the fact\nthat the identity it took for granted cannot remain what it was. Yet this does not prove\nconsciousness caused the transformation; the servant may simply have become conscious of a\nchange produced entirely by physical processes in the brain. Hegel establishes that\nconsciousness can recognize the collapse of its previous understanding, but not yet that\nconsciousness is even partly responsible for what comes next.\nThe servant has felt the ground beneath his previous understanding begin to move, but\nhas not yet discovered what stands in its place. He feels disturbed not because his existence is\nunstable, but because he had been living, unknowingly, on the assumption that it wasn't. This\nraises another question: is consciousness simply experiencing this disruption, or doing\nsomething in response? It could be passively receiving a change the brain has already\nproduced, or actively questioning its previous understanding and forming a new one. Hegel's\nidea of \"absolute negativity\" (5) suggests the second. This term refers to the ability of\nconsciousness to have its entire previous understanding of itself broken down and then move\nbeyond it. In this sense, consciousness works through the change by negating what it previously\naccepted. This does not prove consciousness caused the transformation independently of the\nbrain, but it suggests consciousness is doing more than experiencing the consequences of a\nphysical process; it is participating by confronting and overcoming its own previous\nunderstanding, as Hegel signals by naming \"servile consciousness\" (5) the subject throughout\nthe first paragraph of the passage.\nSo what does the servant do to overcome this? For Hegel, \"the fear of the master is the\nbeginning of wisdom,\" but fear alone cannot form a true conscious mind—work must do more (5). The servant \"forms and shapes the thing\" (5), and unlike the master's immediate\nsatisfaction, this requires \"desire held in check\" (5): resisting gratification to patiently transform\nthe object. Everything up to this point could still be explained as physical processes producing\nphysical actions. Harder to reduce is what the servant makes of the result. Hegel says that\n\"consciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own\nindependence\" (5). The object remains in the world once the servant finishes working on it, and\nhe recognizes that something he produced can exist independently of him. More importantly, he\ntakes this independence as a reflection of himself: by seeing his own work preserved in\nsomething that now exists independently of him, he discovers something about his own ability to\nact in the world. He is not just a passive being determined by circumstance—he has discovered\nsomething about himself through what he has done. This important act is what gives his\n\"individuality…an element of permanence\" (5). Hegel is thus describing more than an action\nfollowed by an outcome; he is describing consciousness interpreting that outcome and using it\nto transform its self-understanding. This does not prove the recognition occurs independently of\nthe brain, but it gives stronger reason to think consciousness participates in the transformation\nrather than just experiencing its results. Additionally, Hegel refers to consciousness as “a\nworker” (5), which strengthens this point. The servant's physical actions may be produced by\nprocesses in the brain, but Hegel also attributes a kind of work to consciousness itself.\nIf Hegel is right, his model depends on consciousness doing more than merely\nundergoing physical changes in the brain. Each stage he describes—fear, work,\npermanence—offers small, ambiguous evidence for both ideas at once. Perhaps the honest\nanswer is that Hegel cannot prove consciousness acts, and neuroscience cannot prove it\ndoesn't. What his account gives us is reason to keep asking, at every stage, whether the\nservant authors his transformation or simply hears about it after his brain has non-consciously\nmade the decision.", "preview": "Selected passage: “For this servile consciousness has been fearful, not of this or that particular thing, or merely at odd moments, but its whole being has been seized with dread. For it has experienced the fear of death—the absol"}, {"id": 15, "title": "Team Build 6 — Collective Paper", "category": "Build", "buildNumber": 6, "content": "Armistice\n\nProf. Hoffman\n\nHonors 241 010 FA26\n\n20 November 2026\n\nBuild 6\n\nSelf-consciousness is portrayed as a process in Phenomenology of Spirit. Hegel argues\n\nthat through interaction this process begins, but it is not solely through reciprocity that one\n\nproduces self-awareness. In the dialectic, the slave becomes self-aware when it acknowledges\n\nfear of death as a constant in its existence. In fact, this shapes the Self by suppressing material\n\ndesires and producing a new awareness through labor. This awareness materializes in the form of\n\nan object. The process of production, as described similarly by Scarry, is how humans project\n\nawareness onto the environment. Thus, in order for consciousness to develop in a permanent\n\nform, it must exist outside the “self” first. In fact, there is no real “Self” until recognition of\n\nindependence through the object takes place. In Hegel’s argument, there is a clear, arbitrary\n\nprocess: interaction with an “Other” to establish recognition, motivation to work through fear of\n\ndeath, and finally the production of a stable form of self through projection.\n\nSo, how does reflexivity work in relation to the development of consciousness? After\n\nexploring Hegel's ideas, it's clear that reflexivity does not spur the discovery of self, but rather is\n\na byproduct of the development of consciousness into self-consciousness. The idea in which the\n\nservant finds meaning in physically changing the world demonstrates this, as while their\n\nconsciousness is developing, they do not understand why or how this is happening. So then, how\n\ncould the servant be reflexive if they don’t truly understand how their consciousness is being\n\naffected by the external world? It was established that reflexivity is not a concrete state of being\n\nbut could rather exist on a spectrum. Our comparison with Milton built on this, revealing that an\n\nawareness of one's own independence doesn’t automatically result in a full understanding of the\n\nexternal forces or internal desires that influence them. This means consciousness may recognize\n\nits independence while failing to fully understand what makes that independence possible.\n\nYet how can a self stay coherent amid constant change? Hegel’s servant and Descartes’s\n\nmeditator both risk losing every part of their old perceived identities. The influence of death and\n\ndoubt reshape their existence. Neither experience settles the outcome. Instead, each regains\n\ncoherence by identifying with something stable. While this stability differs in form, with\n\nDescartes describing a mental cohesion while Hegel describes a materialized continuity. In both\n\nthe self recognizes itself in what endures. A subject, then, must anchor its identity in something\n\noutside itself and recognize independence.\n\nHowever, becoming aware of one’s autonomy does not necessarily mean understanding what\n\nhas shaped it. Comparing this with Milton, we can see that simply recognizing one’s ability to\n\nmake choices does not mean understanding the external influences or internal desires that have\n\ninformed those decisions. This also raises a limitation of reconsolidation. Although individuals\n\ncan revisit and reinterpret past experiences as their understanding of themselves changes, they\n\nmay not recognize all the factors that have influenced their decisions. Reconsolidation can\n\ntherefore change how individuals understand their experiences without necessarily giving them a\n\ncomplete understanding of what has shaped them. In this sense, awareness of one’s own\n\nautonomy does not necessarily mean having complete free will.\n\nThese limitations of self-awareness become even more apparent in social relationships. In the\n\nmaster-slave dialectic, although the slave develops autonomy through labor and production, this\n\ntransformation does not necessarily change how the master perceives the slave or dismantle the\n\nhierarchy between them. Individual self-change alone cannot produce mutual recognition.\n\nDolores makes this problem concrete. Because the language she uses to express freedom was\n\ncreated by her designers, her understanding of that freedom remains limited. Hegel anticipates\n\nthis problem: another consciousness does not simply impose or confirm a definitive identity, yet\n\ndependence does not necessarily produce freedom. The slave’s fear and production, along with\n\nScarry’s objects, show how vulnerability and work can give phenomenality a stable form. Still,\n\nthat form may not be recognized or respected by others. Dolores can transform the language\n\ngiven to her without fully escaping its origin. If mutual recognition is necessary for\n\nreconciliation, how can individuals change their perceptions of one another without disregarding\n\nthe experiences and relationships that shaped them?\n\nReflection is not spontaneous. Reading Hegel and Scarry, we see that reflection is an art\n\ncultivated through suffering, discipline, and creation. If we are driven only by self-preservation,\n\nthen action will be purely instinctive. In order to repress desire, there must be an external force\n\nof resistance. We must either be violently oppressed by a master or have a paradoxical object\n\nimposed upon us by a designer. Although prioritizing suffering may appear problematic,\n\nintentional action arises not from safety, not from privacy, and not from isolation, but rather from\n\nthe interplay between vulnerability, resistance, and creation to change the world."}];
